# Acute Limb Ischaemia - Literature Search on UpToDate

- **Definition** - quickly developing or sudden decreasing in limb perfusion, with new or worsening S/S and often threatening limb viability
- **Etiology of acute limb ischaemia** - acute arterial occlusion:
  - **Native arterial thrombosis** - acute thrombosis can occur in many different pathologies:
    - <u>Acute thrombosis over pre-existing atherosclerotic plaques</u> - due to progressive atherosclerotic narrowing and thrombosis, or intraplaque haemorrhage
    - <u>Acute thrombosis of aneurysm</u> - esp. peripheral aneurysms such as thrombosed popliteal arterial aneurysm
    - <u>Dissection</u> - due to acute dissecting aorta or peripheral artery (e.g. isolated iliac) dissection
    - <u>Hypercoagulability</u> - e.g. malignancy-induced hypercoagulability, anti-phospholipid syndrome, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia
  - **Thromboembolism** (cardiac origin) - thromboemboli typically lodge where there is acute narrowing of the artery, e.g. branch points or over atherosclerotic plaques
  - **Peripheral vasospasm** (non-occlusive arterial ischaemia) - due to Raynaud phenomenon, cardiogenic shock, meningococcal sepsis, vasoconstrictors (e.g. terlipresin)
- **Clinical features of acute limb ischaemia** - clinical presentation depends whether there is a background of chronic arterial insufficiency, which affects <u>collateral vessel development</u>:
  - **Acute-on-chronic ischaemia** - worsening of claudication Sx or rest pain after revascularisation, or manifest as acute pain if distal segments affected
  - **Features of acute limb ischaemia** - consider embolic source esp. if no claudication Hx:
    - <u>Pain</u> - ischaemic pain w/ acute arterial occlusion initially located distally in the extremity, and progresses in severity and proximally, with sudden loss of pain due to ischaemic sensory loss
    - <u>Parasthesia</u> - tingling sensation or numbness attributable to ischaemic nerve dysfunction (sensory deficits over dorsum is the earliest neurological sign)
    - <u>Paralysis</u> - loss of motor function indicative of advanced neurological ischaemia
    - <u>Palor</u> - pallor of the limb (relative to the contralateral limb) with mottling and delayed capillary refill
    - <u>Pulselessness</u> - loss of distal pulses of the affected limb, where presence of distal pulses in the contralateral extremity is suggestive of the absence of chronic occlusive disease and suggests embolus as the etiology
    - <u>Perishing cold</u> - skin is typically cool to the touch
- **Ix and Dx evaluation:**
  - **Doppler USG** - confirm the presence of paedal pulses and listen for arterial and venous signals
  - **Ankle-branchial index** - obtained to assess degree of ischaemia (dependent on site of occlusion) if distal pulses present on Doppler USG
  - **Duplex USG** - simple, non-invasive method of assessing vascular imaging, but provides insufficient anatomical information
  - **Angiography** (CTA/ MRA) - typically indicated for viable or marginally-threatened limb ischaemia
- **Rutherford clinical classification of acute limb ischaemia** - stratification based on viability of the limb: 
![](~/Documents/notes/img/rutherford_classification_acute_limb_ischaemia.png)
  - **Viable or marginally threatened** - no immediate threat of tissue loss, where vascular imaging may be indicated to define extent of disease
  - **Immediatel threatened** - partial motor deficits signifies immediate threat to limbs, requiring urgent revascularisation
  - **Irreversibly ischaemic** - major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage, requiring amputation (revascularsiation for distal segments to permit amputation at lower levels)
